Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters mandated by the Special Act on the Vesting of Property of Pro-Japanese and Anti-National Collaborators in the State and matters necessary for the enforcement thereof.

Article 2 (Procedures and methods for vesting pro-Japanese collaborator's property in the State) (1) If the Investigative Commission on Pro-Japanese Collaborators' Property (hereinafter referred to as the "Commission") under Article 4 of the Special Act on the Vesting of Property of Pro-Japanese and Anti-National Collaborators in the State (hereinafter referred to as the "Act") adopts a resolution on the decision regarding the vesting of the properties of pro-Japanese and anti-national collaborators in the State pursuant to Articles 3 and 7 of the Act on the grounds that such property is the property of a pro-Japanese and anti-national collaborator (hereinafter referred to as "pro-Japanese collaborator's property"), it shall notify the Minister of Finance and Economy thereof. In such cases, materials necessary for the preservation of rights to the property, such as a written resolution, shall be attached thereto. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Dec. 30, 2025>
  (2) Upon receipt of the notification referred to in paragraph (1), the Minister of Finance and Economy shall immediately designate a managing agency. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Dec. 30, 2025>
  (3) The managing agency designated pursuant to paragraph (2) shall, with respect to real estate among pro-Japanese collaborator's property, attach a written resolution, a certified copy of the land register, or a certified copy of the forest register, and request the registration of the "State" as the owner thereof. In such cases, if there is a registration of any right other than the ownership that cannot be set up against the decision of the Commission, a request for the cancellation registration thereof shall also be made.

Article 2-2 (Procedures for preservation of rights to land registered under names of Japanese nationals) (1) The Commission shall investigate land remaining registered under the names of Japanese nationals and, where it decides that any of such land was owned by a Japanese national and is currently occupied by a private person or left abandoned without a managing agency in fact, notify the Minister of Finance and Economy thereof. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Dec. 30, 2025>
  (2) Upon receipt of notification under paragraph (1), the Minister of Finance and Economy shall take measures necessary for preserving rights, including registration, with respect to the relevant land pursuant to statutes and regulations governing State property. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Dec. 30, 2025>
  [This Article Added on Feb. 5, 2008]

Article 3 (Duties of Commission) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 5(1)4 of the Act means the following matters:
  1. Matters concerning measures taken where the vesting of pro-Japanese collaborator's property in the State is impossible pursuant to the proviso of Article 3(1) of the Act;
  2. Matters concerning the preservation, inspection, and duplication of investigation materials, such as reports on the results of investigations pursuant to Article 19 of the Act;
  3. Matters concerning the receipt and processing of objections pursuant to Article 19(8) and Article 21 of the Act;
  4. Detailed matters concerning the procedures and methods of field investigations pursuant to Article 20(3) of the Act.

Article 4 (Duties of standing members) A standing member of the Commission shall assist the Chairperson of the Commission (hereinafter referred to as the "Chairperson") and shall perform the duties of the Commission as delegated by the Chairperson.

Article 5 (Subordinate organizations) The Secretariat pursuant to Article 12 of the Act shall comprise a Planning Bureau and an Investigation Bureau, and shall have a Legal Affairs Officer under the Secretary General. <Amended on Feb. 5, 2008>

Article 6 (Legal Affairs Officer) (1) The position of Legal Affairs Officer shall be filled by a prosecutor.
  (2) The Legal Affairs Officer shall assist the Secretary General with respect to the following matters: <Amended on Feb. 5, 2008>
  1. General supervision of legal affairs and provision of legal counsel for the Commission;
  2. Management and operation of statutes and regulations, and the Commission Rules;
  3. General supervision and conduct of administrative appeals or litigation affairs;
  4. Business affairs concerning the restitution of unjust enrichment derived from pro-Japanese collaborator's property;
  5. Preservative measures following a decision to commence an investigation;
  6. Legal review of investigation result reports, etc.;
  7. Processing of objections;
  8. Other matters relating to legal affairs.

Article 7 (Planning bureau) (1) The position of Director General of the Planning Bureau shall be filled by a public official in general service of the Senior Executive Service.
  (2) The Planning Bureau shall have the Operations Support Division, the Planning and Coordination Division, and the Records Management Division.
  (3) The position of Director of the Operations Support Division shall be filled by a deputy director-general or director-level public official, and the positions of Director of the Planning and Coordination Division and Director of the Records Management Division shall be filled by a director-level public official or a public official in extraordinary civil service equivalent to Grade IV.
  (4) The Operations Support Division shall be responsible for the following matters: <Amended on Feb. 5, 2008>
  1. Personnel affairs, including appointment, service, education and training, pension, remuneration of the staff, operation of the disciplinary committee, and other personnel affairs;
  2. Management of the organization and the prescribed number of personnel of the Commission;
  3. Deleted; <Feb. 5, 2008>
  4. Purchase, procurement, and management of goods;
  5. Operation of revenues and expenditures budgets, settlement of accounts, and accounting;
  6. Internal audits on the Secretariat, and investigation and handling of misconduct by staff members;
  7. Ethics affairs and property registration affairs concerning staff members;
  8. Other matters that do not fall under the jurisdiction of any other division.
  (5) The Planning and Coordination Division shall be responsible for the following matters: <Amended on Feb. 5, 2008>
  1. Overall coordination and adjustment of business affairs of the Commission and the Secretariat;
  2. Holding and operating meetings of the Commission and the Advisory Commission;
  3. General supervision and coordination of matters related to the National Assembly;
  4. General supervision and management of instructions issued by the Chairperson, etc.;
  5. System improvement and operation of the proposal system;
  6. Publicity of the Commission, including policy publicity;
  7. Cooperation with State agencies and the private sector;
  8. Exchange and cooperation with international organizations and relevant foreign agencies regarding property investigations;
  9. Receipt of civil petitions and operation of the civil petition office.
  (6) The Records Management Division shall be responsible for the following matters: <Amended on Feb. 5, 2008>
  1. Business affairs concerning collection, analysis, maintenance, and organization of investigation materials;
  2. Informatization of various materials and establishment of information-sharing systems among relevant agencies;
  3. Matters concerning inspection and provision of materials;
  4. Establishment, management, and operation of the administrative information system of the Commission;
  5. Compilation of white papers;
  6. Document management, including classification, dispatch and receipt, control, and preservation of documents.

Article 8 (Investigation bureau) (1) The position of Director General of the Investigation Bureau shall be filled by a prosecutor, and investigation and research officers shall be placed under the Director General of the Investigation Bureau, and their positions shall be filled by public officials in general or extraordinary civil service belonging to the Senior Executive Service.
  (2) The Investigation Bureau shall have the Investigation Coordination Division, Investigation Division I, Investigation Division II, and Investigation Division III.
  (3) The positions of Director of the Investigation Coordination Division, Director of Investigation Division I, Director of Investigation Division II, and Director of Investigation Division III shall be filled by a director-level public official, a senior investigation officer of the Prosecution Service, or a public official in extraordinary civil service equivalent to Grade IV.
  (4) The Investigation Coordination Division shall be responsible for the following matters:
  1. Planning and coordination of property investigation affairs;
  2. Research and operation of investigation techniques;
  3. Review of whether to apply the investigation results of the Presidential Committee for the Inspection of Collaborations for Japanese Imperialism;
  4. Designating an appraiser and commissioning appraisal.
  5. Collection and analysis of historical materials and academic research outcomes concerning pro-Japanese and anti-national collaborators and pro-Japanese collaborator's property;
  6. Advice and research related to investigations;
  7. Other property investigations and investigation support as directed by the Chairperson.
  (5) Investigation Division I shall be responsible for the following matters:
  1. Property investigation into persons who performed any act under subparagraph 6 or 7 of Article 2 of the Special Act on Finding the Truth regarding Damage from Forced Mobilization under the Japanese Imperialism, and persons falling under subparagraph 1b of Article 2 of the Act;
  2. On-site verification and field investigation regarding the subject property;
  3. Preparation of investigation result reports;
  4. Matters classified by decision of the Commission as falling under the jurisdiction of Investigation Division I.
  (6) Investigation Division II shall be responsible for the following matters:
  1. Property investigation into persons who performed any act under subparagraph 8 or 9 of Article 2 of the Special Act on Finding the Truth regarding Damage from Forced Mobilization under the Japanese Imperialism;
  2. On-site verification and field investigation regarding the subject property;
  3. Preparation of investigation result reports;
  4. Matters classified by decision of the Commission as falling under the jurisdiction of Investigation Division II.
  (7) Investigation Division III shall be responsible for the following matters:
  1. Investigation into land remaining registered under the name of a Japanese national, property for which investigations are requested by local governments and courts, and pro-Japanese collaborator's property registered under the name of "State";
  2. On-site verification and field investigation regarding the subject property;
  3. Preparation of investigation result reports;
  4. Matters classified by decision of the Commission as falling under the jurisdiction of Investigation Division III.
  (8) Investigation and research officers shall assist the Director General of the Investigation Bureau with respect to the matters referred to in paragraph (4).

Article 9 (Temporary adjustment of duties under jurisdiction) When the Chairperson deems it particularly necessary, he or she may temporarily have any division of the Secretariat perform part of the duties of another department. <Amended on Feb. 5, 2008>

Article 10 (Operation of Commission) (1) The Chairperson shall convene and preside over the meetings of the Commission.
  (2) The Commission shall, in cooperation with relevant agencies, formulate security measures for the Commission's offices, communications equipment, etc.

Article 11 (Prescribed number of public officials assigned to Commission) (1) The prescribed number of public officials assigned to the Commission shall be as specified in the Appendix.
  (2) Of the prescribed number of public officials under paragraph (1) and the Appendix, 1 person (1 associate auditor) shall be filled by a public official belonging to the Board of Audit and Inspection; 6 persons (1 director, 2 administrative officers, and 3 administrative senior inspectors) by public officials belonging to the Ministry of Patriots and Veterans Affairs; 2 persons (1 director and 1 administrative officer) by public officials belonging to the Ministry of Finance and Economy; 1 person (1 library senior inspector or archival research officer) by a public official belonging to the Ministry of Education; 8 persons (1 senior investigation officer of the Prosecution Service, 3 prosecution officers or investigation officers, 3 prosecution senior inspectors, and 1 assistant prosecution clerk) by public officials belonging to the Ministry of Justice; 2 persons (1 administrative officer and 1 administrative senior inspector) by public officials belonging to the Ministry of National Defense; 9 persons (1 general service public official belonging to the Senior Executive Service, 1 director or administrative officer, 2 administrative officers, 1 computer operations officer, 1 administrative senior inspector, 1 computer operations senior inspector, 1 library senior inspector, curator researcher, or records research officer, and 1 assistant administrative inspector) by public officials belonging to the Ministry of the Interior and Safety; 2 persons (1 facilities officer and 1 facilities senior inspector) by public officials belonging to the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; 3 persons (3 tax senior inspectors) by public officials belonging to the National Tax Service; 3 persons (1 senior police lieutenant and 2 police lieutenants) by police officers belonging to the National Police Agency; and 5 persons (1 director, administrative officer, or forestry officer, and 4 administrative senior inspectors or forestry senior inspectors) by public officials belonging to the Korea Forest Service. In such cases, the Chairperson shall consult in advance with the head of each relevant agency regarding methods and procedures for such staffing. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017; Apr. 11, 2023; Dec. 30, 2025>
  (3) The Commission shall have 3 prosecutors seconded and assigned to it within the scope of the prescribed number of prosecutors under Article 1 of the Act on the Prescribed Number of Prosecutors. <Added on Feb. 29, 2008>

Article 12 (Expert advisors) When the Commission deems it necessary to efficiently support the business affairs of the Commission and conduct specialized investigation and research work, it may appoint expert advisors and supporting personnel within the limits of the budget.

Article 13 (Allowances) Allowances, travel expenses, etc. may be paid within the limits of the budget to members, expert advisors, witnesses, etc. who attend meetings of the Commission and the Advisory Committee; provided, this shall not apply where a public official attends a meeting in direct connection with the business under his or her jurisdiction.

Article 14 (Procedures and methods for preservative measures) When the Commission files an application for preservative measures under Article 19(1) of the Act, it shall attach explanatory materials, including a written resolution for commencement of investigation.

Article 15 (Methods of notification of commencement of investigation) The methods, etc. for notification under Article 19(6) of the Act shall be governed by Articles 14 through 16 of the Administrative Procedures Act.

Article 16 (Procedures for filing objections to commencement of investigation) (1) A person who intends to file an objection pursuant to Article 19(8) of the Act shall submit to the Commission, in person or by mail, a written objection prescribed by the Commission Rules together with relevant materials.
  (2) Where the Commission deems it necessary, it may hear statements from the person filing the objection.
  (3) Other matters necessary for objections shall be prescribed by the Commission Rules.

Article 17 (Procedures and methods of investigation) (1) If the Commission receives the status of property and relevant materials pursuant to Article 20(1)1 of the Act, it shall prepare a list of such materials.
  (2) In hearing statements pursuant to Article 20(1)2 of the Act, where the Commission deems it necessary, it may have its staff members record the contents of statements or videotape the scene of statements after obtaining consent from the person making the statement.
  (3) Where the Commission intends, pursuant to the latter part of Article 20(3) of the Act, to have the Commission or its staff members hear statements from descendants of pro-Japanese and anti-national collaborators or persons related thereto, it shall notify them in advance of the time, place, purpose, etc. thereof; provided, this shall not apply where statements are heard from persons who voluntarily appear at the field investigation site.
  (4) Where the Commission deems it necessary in connection with investigations under Article 20 of the Act, it may allow relevant experts, etc. to participate therein or hear opinions from persons with relevant knowledge and experience.
  (5) If the Commission determines that relevant evidence or materials are located in a foreign country, it may request the necessary cooperation from the relevant country through the Minister of Foreign Affairs. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (6) Other matters necessary for procedures and methods of investigation into pro-Japanese collaborator's property and land remaining registered under the names of Japanese nationals shall be determined by the Chairperson through a resolution of the Commission. <Added on Feb. 5, 2008>

Article 18 (Procedures for filing objections to field investigations) The provisions of Article 16 shall apply mutatis mutandis to procedures for filing objections pursuant to Article 21(1) of the Act.

Article 19 (Notification of decision) Methods of notification of decisions made by the Commission pursuant to Article 23(1) of the Act and other relevant matters shall be governed by Articles 14 through 16 of the Administrative Procedures Act.

Article 20 (Imposition and collection of administrative fines) (1) When the Chairperson imposes an administrative fine pursuant to Article 29(1) of the Act, he or she shall, after investigating and confirming the relevant violation, send to the person subject to the administrative fine a notice of payment of administrative fine specifying the facts of the violation, the amount of the administrative fine, the payment period, the receiving institution, methods for filing an objection, the objection period, and other relevant matters.
  (2) Administrative fines shall be collected in accordance with procedures for collection of revenue under statutes and regulations governing the management of national funds. In such cases, methods for filing an objection, the objection period, and other relevant matters shall also be stated in the payment notice. <Amended on Feb. 5, 2008>

Article 21 (Detailed operating rules) Detailed matters necessary for the enforcement of this Decree shall be determined by the Chairperson upon a resolution of the Commission.

Article 22 (Period of existence of the Secretariat) The Secretariat shall continue to exist until 3 months after the termination of activities of the Commission. <Amended on Feb. 5, 2008>
